{
  "claim_id": "351acde4-828d-43f8-a6a9-eb33d64391e4",
  "text": "The memory burden effect halts black hole evaporation partway, stabilizing the hole.",
  "claim_type": "empirical_derived",
  "lifecycle_state": "active",
  "decomposition_status": "complete",
  "created_at": "2026-09-18T17:56:47.967Z",
  "assessment": {
    "status": "contested",
    "confidence": 0.85,
    "reasoning": "The claim splits into a mechanism, that a black hole's information load backreacts on the evaporation rate, and a prediction, that the hole stabilizes after roughly half its entropy is radiated rather than evaporating away. The memory-burden program supports the mechanism from prototype systems, saturon bound states and solitons, where decay demonstrably slows as information content is lost; the black hole case is then reached by a correspondence argument, [[claim:fffa4140-98b7-442c-83e0-571cd5bace21|that decay suppression is generic to saturons]], together with [[claim:e87197ac-dd0d-4bff-86fd-b9c8abb7527e|the prediction that burdened lifetimes scale as a high power of the initial entropy]]. No accepted derivation exists for the black hole itself: the program's own papers state that what happens after the transition is not known and rests on prototype evidence, and the lifetime formula's exponent is fixed by analogy rather than calculation.\n\nThe contrary case is concrete rather than a mere default to orthodoxy. A holographic space-time analysis computes the emission rate directly and finds no suppression, because the phase space opened for radiation outweighs the entropy cost of the frozen degrees of freedom, [[claim:64a62fad-0d7d-4f1d-a47e-3f8d62ef5955|denying that the effect arises in holographic models]]. Separately, analyses of big bang nucleosynthesis and the cosmic microwave background find that light primordial black holes keep radiating through the epochs that constrain them unless [[claim:bb218573-b19d-49ae-b1e3-87fe8e234032|the semiclassical-to-burdened transition is practically instantaneous]], which under any smoother transition removes [[claim:d0548ba9-b0d8-48e7-969e-e7d1706eca82|the proposed primordial black hole dark matter window]]. These results constrain a consequence of the conjecture more directly than the mechanism itself, and the holographic result is framework-internal, which is why the disagreement is live on both sides rather than settled against the newcomers.\n\nTaken together the evidence supports treating the conjecture as a live, testable proposal rather than an established result: the prototype evidence is real, the cosmological constraint pressure is real, and the two sides disagree in top journals. What would move the verdict is a derivation of the emission rate for the black hole case under stated assumptions, a demonstration that suppression appears or fails generically across quantum-gravity frameworks, or a computation of the transition's shape, which is the recognized crux of the phenomenological debate. A related but distinct account of stabilization, [[claim:06c90251-420a-48a3-8724-4c110c7c8094|remnant formation near the Planck scale]], competes at the endpoint rather than partway and is recorded as a see-also."
  }
}
